ES6 Features -
ES6 stands for ECMAScript 6.
ECMAScript (ES) is the official standard or specification on which JavaScript is based.
6 represents the 6th version of this standard.
ES6 was released in 2015, so it is also called ECMAScript 2015 (ES2015).
ES6 is the 6th version of JavaScript that introduced many new features to make coding easier, shorter, and more powerful.
Why was ES6 introduced?
Before ES6, JavaScript code was often long and repetitive. ES6 introduced new features that help developers write cleaner and more readable code.

1. Template Literals (` `)
Template Literals allow us to create strings easily using backticks ( ) instead of single (' ') or double (" ") quotes.
They also allow us to insert variables directly inside the string using ${ }.
Before ES6
let name = "Rahul";
let age = 22;
console.log("My name is " + name + " and I am " + age + " years old.");

Output
My name is Rahul and I am 22 years old.

Using Template Literals
let name = "Rahul";
let age = 22;
console.log(`My name is ${name} and I am ${age} years old.`);
Output
My name is Rahul and I am 22 years old.

Multi-line String
let message = `
Welcome
to
JavaScript
`;
console.log(message);
Output
Welcome
to
JavaScript
Advantages
No need for +
Easy to insert variables
Supports multiple lines
2. Spread Operator (...)
Spread Operator expands an array or object into individual elements.
Example 1
let numbers = [10,20,30];

console.log(...numbers);
Output
10 20 30

Example 2: Copy Array
let arr1 = [1,2,3];

let arr2 = [...arr1];

console.log(arr2);
Output
[1,2,3]

Example 3: Merge Arrays
let a = [1,2];
let b = [3,4];

let c = [...a,...b];

console.log(c);
Output
[1,2,3,4]

Example 4: Object Copy
let student = {
    name:"Aman",
    age:20
};
let copy = {...student};
console.log(copy);


Output -
{ name: "Aman", age: 20 } 

Uses
Copy arrays
Merge arrays
Copy objects
Merge objects

3. Rest Operator (...)
Rest Operator collects multiple values into a single array.
Rest = Collect Remaining Values
Example
function show(...numbers){
    console.log(numbers);
}

show(10,20,30,40);
Output
[10,20,30,40]
Another Example
function total(a,b,...others){
    console.log(a);
    console.log(b);
    console.log(others);
}

total(10,20,30,40,50);
Output
10
20
[30,40,50]

Difference
Spread → Expands

Rest → Collects


4. Destructuring
Destructuring means taking values from an array or object and storing them into separate variables easily.
Array Destructuring
Without Destructuring
let colors = ["Red","Blue","Green"];

let first = colors[0];
let second = colors[1];

Using Destructuring
let colors = ["Red","Blue","Green"];

let [first, second] = colors;

console.log(first);
console.log(second);
Output
Red
Blue

Skip Values
let numbers = [10,20,30];

let [a,,c] = numbers;

console.log(a);
console.log(c);
Output
10
30
Object Destructuring
let student = {
    name:"Aman",
    age:21,
    city:"Delhi"
};

let {name, age} = student;

console.log(name);
console.log(age);
Output
Aman
21

Rename Variable
let student = {
    name:"Aman"
};

let {name:studentName} = student;

console.log(studentName);
Output
Aman

Advantages
Less code
Easy to read
Easy to extract data


5. Default Parameters
Default Parameters allow us to give a default value to function parameters.
If the user does not pass a value, the default value is used.
Without Default Parameter
function greet(name){
    console.log("Hello " + name);
}

greet();
Output
Hello undefined

With Default Parameter
function greet(name="Guest"){
    console.log("Hello " + name);
}

greet();
Output
Hello Guest

Passing Value
function greet(name="Guest"){
    console.log("Hello " + name);
}

greet("Rahul");
Output
Hello Rahul

Real-Life Example
function order(item="Burger", quantity=1){
    console.log(`Item: ${item}`);
    console.log(`Quantity: ${quantity}`);
}

order();
Output
Item: Burger
Quantity: 1

order("Pizza",2);
Output
Item: Pizza
Quantity: 2




